WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.976
Your friend is hiking alone, and she promised to text you "all good" every half hour.

2
00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:09.753
An hour passes.

3
00:00:09.753 --> 00:00:10.581
Nothing.

4
00:00:11.033 --> 00:00:12.058
Maybe she fell.

5
00:00:12.058 --> 00:00:14.722
Maybe she's in a valley with no signal.

6
00:00:14.722 --> 00:00:16.361
Maybe her phone is flat.

7
00:00:16.361 --> 00:00:20.255
From your sofa, all three look exactly the same: silence.

8
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:22.307
So when do you call rescue?

9
00:00:22.307 --> 00:00:26.250
Call too soon, and a helicopter finds her eating a sandwich.

10
00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:28.681
Call too late, and help arrives late.

11
00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:31.989
Put the hike into a payments app.

12
00:00:31.989 --> 00:00:34.605
One machine records every café's payments.

13
00:00:34.605 --> 00:00:40.087
A second machine watches it, ready to hand its work to a spare, the standby, if it dies.

14
00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:43.295
The watcher can't see inside the first machine.

15
00:00:43.295 --> 00:00:44.808
All it sees is messages.

16
00:00:44.808 --> 00:00:49.093
So the first machine sends a small "I'm alive" message every second.

17
00:00:49.093 --> 00:00:52.307
That's called a heartbeat, like your friend's text.

18
00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:53.635
Map it out loud.

19
00:00:53.635 --> 00:00:55.495
Your friend is the machine.

20
00:00:55.495 --> 00:00:57.286
Her text is the heartbeat.

21
00:00:57.286 --> 00:00:59.628
You, on the sofa, are the watcher.

22
00:00:59.628 --> 00:01:02.039
And the rescue team is the standby.

23
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:05.899
Now watch the silence: the time since the last heartbeat.

24
00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:09.119
It grows, and each heartbeat drops it back to zero.

25
00:01:09.119 --> 00:01:11.771
A healthy machine is never quiet for long.

26
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:17.681
So the watcher keeps one rule: if nothing has arrived for T seconds, suspect the machine.

27
00:01:17.681 --> 00:01:22.887
A program with a rule like that is called a failure detector, and T is its timeout.

28
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.025
The only question is how big T should be.

29
00:01:26.025 --> 00:01:27.885
And neither answer is free.

30
00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:31.129
Try a long timeout first: ten seconds.

31
00:01:31.129 --> 00:01:32.767
Now the machine dies.

32
00:01:32.767 --> 00:01:35.049
The watcher waits, ten whole seconds.

33
00:01:35.049 --> 00:01:39.921
Meanwhile payments keep arriving, two thousand a second, and nobody takes them.

34
00:01:39.921 --> 00:01:43.560
By the time it acts, twenty thousand payments are stranded.

35
00:01:43.833 --> 00:01:46.515
Only then does the standby take over.

36
00:01:46.515 --> 00:01:49.052
That step has a name too: failover.

37
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:50.480
So make it short.

38
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.301
Say one and a half seconds.

39
00:01:52.301 --> 00:01:54.324
The machine is fine this time.

40
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.611
But one heartbeat gets stuck on the way.

41
00:01:56.733 --> 00:01:58.010
The silence passes T.

42
00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:02.750
The watcher declares a live machine dead, and the standby takes over its work.

43
00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:05.607
But the first machine is still doing that work.

44
00:02:05.867 --> 00:02:10.652
Two machines, one job: a whole machine's worth of payments, charged twice.

45
00:02:10.933 --> 00:02:11.381
Well...

46
00:02:11.381 --> 00:02:14.453
the helicopter just found her eating a sandwich.

47
00:02:14.453 --> 00:02:17.204
That wrong verdict is called a false alarm.

48
00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:18.380
And it spreads.

49
00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:23.370
The moved work slows other machines' heartbeats, and the next false alarm follows.

50
00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:26.596
A group can talk itself into declaring everyone dead.

51
00:02:26.867 --> 00:02:28.287
This happens for real.

52
00:02:28.287 --> 00:02:40.292
Elasticsearch, a search program that runs on a group of machines, split itself in two on about one start in ten, because its machines were sometimes slower than its three second timeout.

53
00:02:40.292 --> 00:02:41.905
Fifteen seconds fixed it.

54
00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:46.374
Wouldn't it be nice if the watcher could just tell slow from dead?

55
00:02:46.374 --> 00:02:46.952
It can't.

56
00:02:46.952 --> 00:02:52.927
From where it sits, a slow heartbeat and a dead machine send exactly the same thing: nothing.

57
00:02:53.167 --> 00:02:55.733
A timeout detects silence, not death.

58
00:02:56.533 --> 00:02:58.022
So we pick T from data.

59
00:02:58.022 --> 00:03:02.553
Every time a heartbeat lands, write down the gap since the one before.

60
00:03:02.553 --> 00:03:04.041
Then stack the gaps up.

61
00:03:04.067 --> 00:03:09.897
Start with a calm link inside one building, where each heartbeat takes one to five milliseconds.

62
00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.600
Five minutes of gaps, and every one lands within a few milliseconds of one second.

63
00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:19.332
With T at one and a half seconds, no gap reaches it: no false alarms.

64
00:03:19.332 --> 00:03:24.407
And when the machine dies, the watcher notices in about one and a half seconds.

65
00:03:24.667 --> 00:03:33.057
Now a jittery link between two regions far apart, where each heartbeat takes anywhere from 30 to 830 milliseconds.

66
00:03:33.067 --> 00:03:35.674
Same timeout, one and a half seconds.

67
00:03:35.674 --> 00:03:38.423
In five minutes, how many false alarms?

68
00:03:38.423 --> 00:03:39.551
Pause and guess.

69
00:03:42.833 --> 00:03:43.462
Fifteen.

70
00:03:43.462 --> 00:03:45.897
On a machine that never failed.

71
00:03:46.167 --> 00:03:46.933
Here's why.

72
00:03:46.933 --> 00:03:51.116
A gap is one second, plus the difference between two delays.

73
00:03:51.116 --> 00:03:56.832
One heartbeat comes fast, the next one slow, and a gap can stretch to 1.8 seconds.

74
00:03:57.067 --> 00:03:59.463
Every gap past T is a false alarm.

75
00:03:59.463 --> 00:04:03.480
And every bit of T is time a dead machine goes unnoticed.

76
00:04:03.733 --> 00:04:05.717
So move T out to three seconds.

77
00:04:05.717 --> 00:04:07.317
Now both links are quiet.

78
00:04:07.317 --> 00:04:12.628
But the calm link waits three seconds to notice a crash, twice as long as it needs.

79
00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:13.767
Side by side.

80
00:04:13.767 --> 00:04:20.501
At one and a half seconds, the calm link is perfect and the jittery link raises fifteen false alarms.

81
00:04:20.501 --> 00:04:25.101
At three seconds, both are quiet, and the calm link is twice as slow.

82
00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:30.416
Remember the tempting plan for the hike: one deadline, say an hour, for every walk.

83
00:04:30.416 --> 00:04:34.431
But she's never late in the city, and often late in the mountains.

84
00:04:34.431 --> 00:04:36.864
One number can't suit both links either.

85
00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.120
So, a few rules.

86
00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:45.007
Measure the gaps over days and over many machines, and put T beyond the gaps you see when things are normal.

87
00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:52.780
And if one link needs a quick verdict and the other needs patience, you need a timeout that learns what normal looks like.

88
00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:53.761
That comes next.

89
00:04:54.033 --> 00:04:55.659
So, when do you call rescue?

90
00:04:55.659 --> 00:04:59.551
When the silence is longer than anything you've measured as normal.

91
00:04:59.833 --> 00:05:02.400
A timeout detects silence, not death.

92
00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:04.236
One quick check.

93
00:05:04.236 --> 00:05:09.934
Heartbeats come every second on the calm link, T is three seconds, and the machine dies.

94
00:05:09.934 --> 00:05:13.689
At the very least, how long until the watcher suspects it?

95
00:05:13.689 --> 00:05:14.725
Pause and think.

96
00:05:17.967 --> 00:05:18.909
Three seconds.

97
00:05:18.909 --> 00:05:23.151
The timeout itself is the least time any crash takes to notice.

98
00:05:23.151 --> 00:05:25.709
That's the price of every second of T.
